WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.033 --> 00:00:02.133
-It is great to have you here.

00:00:02.133 --> 00:00:03.200
Thanks so much for coming on.

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:04.501
-Thanks for finally
having me on.

00:00:04.501 --> 00:00:06.100
-I can't believe that you've
never been --

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:07.434
-I know.
I feel like we have, too.

00:00:07.434 --> 00:00:08.834
-Maybe on "Late Night"
or something.

00:00:08.834 --> 00:00:10.267
We have.
We've hung out or something.

00:00:10.267 --> 00:00:11.868
-Yeah, yeah, yeah. Something.
-I know you.

00:00:11.868 --> 00:00:13.734
But maybe it's just because
I'm a fan.

00:00:13.734 --> 00:00:16.133
-Me too. I know.
That's strange how that happens.

00:00:16.133 --> 00:00:20.367
-You have an amazing history,
though, with "The Tonight Show."

00:00:20.367 --> 00:00:23.667
-Why, yes, I do.
-I love this story.

00:00:23.667 --> 00:00:26.300
-Yes.
-Is it your -- Who was it, now?

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:27.634
This is your grandfather?

00:00:27.634 --> 00:00:31.667
-My grandfather was a comedian
called Cliff Arquette,

00:00:31.667 --> 00:00:33.767
but he had a character he
invented called Charley Weaver.

00:00:33.767 --> 00:00:35.434
-I mean, Charley Weaver.

00:00:35.434 --> 00:00:37.234
I totally remember
Charley Weaver.

00:00:37.234 --> 00:00:40.033
That is Jack Paar.

00:00:40.033 --> 00:00:42.534
That's your grandfather,
Cliff Arquette.

00:00:42.534 --> 00:00:45.033
This is "The Tonight Show,"
1959.

00:00:45.033 --> 00:00:46.400
-Yes, exactly.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:48.534
-And this is at
the Hudson Theatre.

00:00:48.534 --> 00:00:50.000
But that's wild.
-Yeah.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.400
-Do you remember telling
stories about these days?

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:54.133
-Well, my grandpa was
in Vaudeville.

00:00:54.133 --> 00:00:56.367
And when he was 14,
he went around the country

00:00:56.367 --> 00:00:58.767
playing at speakeasies,
the trumpet.

00:00:58.767 --> 00:01:00.634
And then he did live radio.

00:01:00.634 --> 00:01:02.934
He was Slap-happy Grandpappy
on these, like,

00:01:02.934 --> 00:01:04.467
live radio comedy shows.

00:01:04.467 --> 00:01:05.501
-Isn't that wild?

00:01:05.501 --> 00:01:06.767
-And then he retired,

00:01:06.767 --> 00:01:09.100
but he'd created this
character, Charley Weaver.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:11.267
And everyone was watching
"The Jack Paar Show,"

00:01:11.267 --> 00:01:13.501
because it was just this
huge phenomena.

00:01:13.501 --> 00:01:15.634
And he was watching one night,
and Jack said,

00:01:15.634 --> 00:01:17.400
"You know who used to
crack me up?

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:20.334
Charley Weaver.
What happened to him?

00:01:20.334 --> 00:01:21.534
What happened to
Charley Weaver?"

00:01:21.534 --> 00:01:22.934
And my grandpa
had been watching,

00:01:22.934 --> 00:01:25.567
so he wrote him,
typed him a letter.

00:01:25.567 --> 00:01:28.234
"I'm alive. I'm good.
I'm retired.

00:01:28.234 --> 00:01:30.300
Thanks for thinking of me.
I love your show."

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:31.834
And, so, Jack Paar
wrote him back and said,

00:01:31.834 --> 00:01:33.133
"Why don't you come on my show."

00:01:33.133 --> 00:01:35.367
And then he became
a semi-regular.

00:01:35.367 --> 00:01:36.767
And then he got
"Hollywood Squares,"

00:01:36.767 --> 00:01:38.734
and he had this
whole second career.

00:01:38.734 --> 00:01:39.834
-Isn't that amazing?
-Yeah.

00:01:39.834 --> 00:01:42.167
-I love that.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:01:42.167 --> 00:01:45.133
-So, did your parents encourage
you to get into show business?

00:01:45.133 --> 00:01:47.167
Because --
-No. My mom wanted to cry.

00:01:47.167 --> 00:01:49.167
She was like, "Please!

00:01:49.167 --> 00:01:52.033
Please! Your principal said
you could be a doctor.

00:01:52.033 --> 00:01:53.467
Why are you doing this?"

00:01:53.467 --> 00:01:55.634
-"Your principal said --" Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:01:55.634 --> 00:01:57.300
-But I read somewhere
that you did this.

00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:59.267
You said,
"I'm going to try acting.

00:01:59.267 --> 00:02:00.667
I'm going to give it a year.

00:02:00.667 --> 00:02:03.334
And if I don't do it in a year,
I'm not going to do it."

00:02:03.334 --> 00:02:05.300
But what was plan B?
Do you --

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:07.801
-I was going to be a midwife.
Yeah, that was my plan B.

00:02:07.801 --> 00:02:08.934
-That was your plan B?
-Yeah.

00:02:08.934 --> 00:02:10.601
And it's --
You know, someday, I'll still --

00:02:10.601 --> 00:02:11.601
maybe I'll be a midwife.

00:02:11.601 --> 00:02:12.968
Who knows?

00:02:12.968 --> 00:02:14.534
-Yeah, I mean, I think acting is
going pretty well for you.

00:02:14.534 --> 00:02:15.734
-It is. Thank you.
-Yeah.

00:02:15.734 --> 00:02:17.100
-Thank you, Lord. But -- Yeah.

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:18.701
-But what was that year like?

00:02:18.701 --> 00:02:20.267
Would you audition and just --

00:02:20.267 --> 00:02:23.300
-Yeah. Well, the thing is,
I love both these ideas,

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:25.467
but I was actually
shy and scared.

00:02:25.467 --> 00:02:26.667
"What if I'm a bad actress?"

00:02:26.667 --> 00:02:28.901
or "I don't want to be doing
this for 30 years

00:02:28.901 --> 00:02:30.234
and never get anywhere.

00:02:30.234 --> 00:02:32.801
So I'm going to give myself
one year,

00:02:32.801 --> 00:02:36.901
because what I want
more than being a midwife

00:02:36.901 --> 00:02:39.501
or being an actress,
to be a brave person.

00:02:39.501 --> 00:02:41.167
So I'm going to say,
for one year,

00:02:41.167 --> 00:02:43.400
I'm going to go on this
quest to be brave.

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:46.701
So, every day, since it's more
frightening for me

00:02:46.701 --> 00:02:49.534
to try to be an actress,
I'm going to try to be

00:02:49.534 --> 00:02:53.033
an actress every day for a year
and be willing to fail."

00:02:53.033 --> 00:02:55.667
So, I'd go on
these auditions sometimes.

00:02:55.667 --> 00:02:57.567
I went on this audition.
It went so bad.

00:02:57.567 --> 00:02:59.067
And they called
my agent afterwards.

00:02:59.067 --> 00:03:00.901
And they were like,
"She was horrible!

00:03:00.901 --> 00:03:03.033
I mean, that was the
worst audition I ever saw.

00:03:03.033 --> 00:03:05.534
Oh, my --
What was she thinking?"

00:03:05.534 --> 00:03:08.267
And I was like, "Yeah!
I'm so brave!

00:03:08.267 --> 00:03:09.567
I'm going out again.

00:03:09.567 --> 00:03:12.400
-I'm doing my brave --
I'm doing my year of brave.

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:14.100
-Well, I also said to them,
"Excuse me.

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:16.434
That was no part at all."

00:03:16.434 --> 00:03:17.734
It was, like,
this dumb secretary part.

00:03:17.734 --> 00:03:18.868
She did nothing.

00:03:18.868 --> 00:03:21.634
So, in the audition,
I was like, "Oh, hi.

00:03:21.634 --> 00:03:24.434
Oh, Mr. Grant.

00:03:24.434 --> 00:03:26.400
Oh, I think I dropped my papers.
I'm sorry."

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:27.501
You know.
-Yeah.

00:03:27.501 --> 00:03:29.400
-I just made her really
fumbly-bumbly,

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:30.801
and they thought
that's who I was

00:03:30.801 --> 00:03:32.534
and that I just wasn't prepared.

00:03:32.534 --> 00:03:33.801
But I was like, "Hell no.

00:03:33.801 --> 00:03:35.968
Call them back and tell them
that was a choice."

00:03:35.968 --> 00:03:37.968
-Yeah! "I made that part
interesting."

00:03:37.968 --> 00:03:39.701
-Exactly.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:39.701 --> 00:03:43.367
And, obviously, I did a good
job, because they were pissed.

00:03:43.367 --> 00:03:46.400
-They were -- Yeah.
Mission accomplished.

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:47.834
-Exactly.

00:03:47.834 --> 00:03:50.667
-"Severance" season finale airs
Friday on Apple TV+.

00:03:50.667 --> 00:03:52.901
-Yeah, and we just got picked up
for a second season.

00:03:52.901 --> 00:03:54.601
-You did? Oh!

00:03:54.601 --> 00:03:56.234
Come on, now!
-Right.

00:03:56.234 --> 00:03:58.868
-That's fantastic. I love that.
-Waffle party!

00:03:58.868 --> 00:04:00.434
-Yes!
-At my house. No.

00:04:00.434 --> 00:04:02.634
-It's a great concept.

00:04:02.634 --> 00:04:06.300
I remember we had Ben here to
talk about it, Ben Stiller,

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:07.834
who directed
most of the episodes.

00:04:07.834 --> 00:04:10.667
But it's trippy
and it's really good.

00:04:10.667 --> 00:04:13.300
It's kind of like --
How would we describe it?

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:17.167
It's not -- It's, like, a dry --
It's, like, a dark comedy,

00:04:17.167 --> 00:04:20.100
but it's also -- It's not
sci-fi, but kind of is.

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:21.300
-Well, yeah, exactly.

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:23.801
When we were shooting,
I was asking him, too,

00:04:23.801 --> 00:04:25.400
"What's the tone here?"

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:28.801
And I was like, "Is this a retro
sci-fi soap opera?

00:04:28.801 --> 00:04:30.400
Is this -- What is this?

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:34.200
Is this --"
At moments, it's film noir.

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:37.567
Then it's comedy.
I mean, it was really tricky.

00:04:37.567 --> 00:04:39.567
But Ben knew the tone
the whole time.

00:04:39.567 --> 00:04:41.868
-You can get a thing
implanted in your brain

00:04:41.868 --> 00:04:44.300
so that when you
clock into work,

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:46.801
all you remember is
your workday,

00:04:46.801 --> 00:04:48.634
and then,
once you clock out of work,

00:04:48.634 --> 00:04:51.167
all you remember is
your outside workday.

00:04:51.167 --> 00:04:52.968
-Yeah, you can't remember
anything at work

00:04:52.968 --> 00:04:55.467
when you're home
and you can't remember anything

00:04:55.467 --> 00:04:56.734
at home when you're at work.

00:04:56.734 --> 00:04:58.734
-Exactly.
We really explained it --

00:04:58.734 --> 00:05:00.367
Where were you
when we had Ben on?

00:05:00.367 --> 00:05:02.734
I was, like, fumbling.
That's perfect. We nailed it.

00:05:02.734 --> 00:05:06.033
I didn't think it was that easy
to describe, but yes.

00:05:06.033 --> 00:05:07.467
And it's really good
and beautifully shot

00:05:07.467 --> 00:05:08.667
and it's trippy.

00:05:08.667 --> 00:05:11.133
You're so great in it.
I want to show a clip.

00:05:11.133 --> 00:05:13.234
Here's Patricia Arquette
in "Severance."

00:05:13.234 --> 00:05:14.667
Take a look at this.

00:05:14.667 --> 00:05:18.434
-It's not your job to play
nursemaid to every new refiner.

00:05:18.434 --> 00:05:22.300
-Okay. So, what is my job?

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:24.367
-Are you really asking me that?

00:05:24.367 --> 00:05:26.767
-Yeah.

00:05:26.767 --> 00:05:29.534
What is it we actually do here?

00:05:31.234 --> 00:05:34.567
-We serve Kier, you child!

00:05:34.567 --> 00:05:39.267
And until you get that through
your mildewed little brain

00:05:39.267 --> 00:05:40.801
and hit quota,

00:05:40.801 --> 00:05:45.801
MDR's hallway privileges
are hereby revoked.

00:05:45.801 --> 00:05:48.234
So get your little ass
back to your desk

00:05:48.234 --> 00:05:51.300
and stay there
until you're told to move.

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:55.200
♪♪

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:58.267
-Yes! Come on!
-Ooh! Scary lady.

00:05:58.267 --> 00:06:00.834
-That's how you do it!

00:06:00.834 --> 00:06:03.734
That is how you do it.

00:06:03.734 --> 00:06:05.200
I'm so happy you're here.

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:06.767
You know,
we were talking backstage,

00:06:06.767 --> 00:06:08.701
and you mentioned
that there's something

00:06:08.701 --> 00:06:10.367
you want to bring awareness to.

00:06:10.367 --> 00:06:12.367
And I would like
to talk about it.

00:06:12.367 --> 00:06:14.334
-Thank you so much.

00:06:14.334 --> 00:06:16.100
You know, I try to follow the

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:17.767
Center for Missing and
Endangered Kids.

00:06:17.767 --> 00:06:20.834
And on my Twitter feed
came up this little boy,

00:06:20.834 --> 00:06:21.834
Simjie Morrison.

00:06:21.834 --> 00:06:23.033
He's 12 years old.

00:06:23.033 --> 00:06:26.234
He has no known family,
no known relatives,

00:06:26.234 --> 00:06:28.334
and he went missing
in Philadelphia.

00:06:28.334 --> 00:06:31.501
But because he's under --
he's over 10

00:06:31.501 --> 00:06:33.467
and they don't know
he was abducted,

00:06:33.467 --> 00:06:35.400
they never put out
an Amber Alert.

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:37.734
So, he went missing in Philly.

00:06:37.734 --> 00:06:40.767
He's very small.
He only weighs 72 pounds.

00:06:40.767 --> 00:06:42.167
He's 5 feet tall.

00:06:42.167 --> 00:06:45.968
Please look at the Center for
Missing &amp; Endangered Kids

00:06:45.968 --> 00:06:47.033
and look at his picture.

00:06:47.033 --> 00:06:48.534
Simjie Morrison.

00:06:48.534 --> 00:06:51.834
And if you happen to see him --
New York, Philly, New Jersey,

00:06:51.834 --> 00:06:54.701
call 1-800-THE-LOST.

00:06:54.701 --> 00:06:56.133
-1-800-THE-LOST.

00:06:56.133 --> 00:06:58.601
Do we know where
he was seen last?

00:06:58.601 --> 00:07:01.033
-He was seen in Philly.

00:07:01.033 --> 00:07:03.267
He went missing from
this sort of

00:07:03.267 --> 00:07:06.567
foster-care facility called
Forget Me Knot.

00:07:06.567 --> 00:07:09.167
-Guys, if anyone knows
anything about this,

00:07:09.167 --> 00:07:10.501
call 1-800-THE-LOST.

00:07:10.501 --> 00:07:13.267
And I appreciate you bringing
that to our attention,

00:07:13.267 --> 00:07:15.100
and maybe somebody --
That'd be amazing.

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:17.367
-Yeah, please.
-Patricia Arquette, everyone.

00:07:17.367 --> 00:07:21.133
The season finale of "Severance"
airs this Friday on Apple TV+.

